417] [17592186050379 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050379 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050379 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050379 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050379 #edb/ref 1031 "Improper Integrals of the Second Kind" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050379 #edb/ref 1033 759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050379 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "445c7063-c967-5f21-b424-ab8820e310ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050628 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050628 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050628 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050628 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050628 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050628 #edb/ref 1031 "Integrating Algebraic Functions Using Substitution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050628 #edb/ref 1033 334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050628 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5a0d0fb8-c0a3-55dc-a497-9f0d5f0eda76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050675 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050675 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050675 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050675 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050675 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050675 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050675 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050675 #edb/ref 1031 "Differentiating Inverse Trigonometric Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050675 #edb/ref 1033 303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050675 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5e05a47e-5905-5eee-b6ea-48c720a8d288" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064090 #edb/ref 1069 "e-470" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064090 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064090 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "48ec6930-88b7-5aad-9bb7-c5e23e766f80" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064090 #edb/ref 1074 "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $9x^2 = (3x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\,\\textrm d u=\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(3x)^{2}}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡3x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064091 #edb/ref 1069 "e-471" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064091 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the integral  $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x }.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064091 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e89a278-54ab-59a6-adb6-38f43c0fbe9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064091 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int \\dfrac{3}{2(1 + 25x^2)} = \\dfrac 3 2 \\int \\dfrac{1}{1+25x^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^2}\\textrm{d}u =\\arctan u +C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $25x^2 = (5x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 5\\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{2+50{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(5x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡5x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064092 #edb/ref 1069 "e-472" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064092 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt 2} \\dfrac{1}{2+x^2}\\, \\textrm{d}x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064092 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb771ff3-b5a9-5900-913f-df23957760b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064092 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2(1+{x}^{2}/2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2(1+(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this integral now resembles the basic integral for the inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{1}{1+u^2}\\,\\textrm d u = \\arctan{u} + C.\n$$\n\nWe make the substitution $u=\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt 2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\sqrt 2 \\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064101 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5014" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064101 #edb/ref 1071 "${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{5\\, \\textrm{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^2-1}}  = }$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064101 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56860611-869d-5035-bf8f-f568c774d78d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064101 #edb/ref 1074 "This resembles the basic integral for $\\textrm{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^2-1}} \\textrm{d}u = \\textrm{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nLet $u = 5x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n5\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5\\,\\text{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^{2}-1}} & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(u)+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(5x)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064218 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064218 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064218 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064218 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064218 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064218 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 175921862501