0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428006 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186428004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428006 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114039" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428006 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428006 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{1+6{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428006 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "276f25e8-0fe6-5cab-8eeb-f55147c6c339" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428006 #edb/ref 1074 "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $6{x}^{2}=(\\sqrt{6}x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{6}x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{6}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1+6{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+(\\sqrt{6}x)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{6}x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428007 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186428005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428007 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114075" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428007 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428007 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428007 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bacbcea3-6ad6-5840-9bf7-1e8371e0974d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428007 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/9)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/9}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/3)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/3)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
